\cls{} and \traj{} both scale positively from 4B to 8B (+1.27\pp{} and +1.70\pp{} respectively), while \cwm{} remains flat ($-$0.03\pp{}). At 8B, the ranking is \cls{} (89.94) $>$ \traj{} (87.70) $>$ \cwm{} (87.33). The \cls{}--\cwm{} gap widens from 1.32\pp{} at 4B to 2.62\pp{} at 8B (Figure~\ref{fig:scaling_gap}); clustered bootstrap on the 3-seed superset (42, 123, 456 for \cls{}/\cwm{}) yields $p = 0.0066$ (95\% CI $[1.80, 2.71]$), which confirms the widening is not a sampling artifact.

\cwm{} stagnation ($-$0.03\pp{}) may reflect suboptimal hyperparameter transfer at 8B rather than a fundamental limitation; we do not claim generation \emph{cannot} benefit from scale. The interaction between formulation and scale amplifies at repository-level complexity (\S\ref{sec:repo}), where \traj{} also shows no scaling benefit (72.92\% at both 4B and 8B); only \cls{} scales positively across both complexity levels.


\subsection{Per-Test vs.\ Trajectory-Level Equivalence}
\label{sec:equivalence}

We compare \cls{} (aggregated via all-pass rule) against \traj{} on 523 variant-level instances across five seeds. The pooled gap is $-$0.45\pp{} (95\% CI [$-$1.71, +0.84]); the TOST equivalence test confirms statistical equivalence at $p = 0.007$ ($\pm$2\pp{} margin), with a minimum equivalence margin of $\pm$1.63\pp{}. The $\pm$2\pp{} margin reflects practical significance: formulation differences below 2\pp{} are unlikely to affect deployment decisions where absolute accuracy dominates the cost-benefit analysis. Within-problem predictions are correlated (intraclass correlation ICC $= 0.191$, $p < 0.0001$, design effect (DEFF) $= 3.97$); all statistical tests use clustered bootstrap accordingly. On DeepSeek-Coder, the observed \cls{}--\cwm{} gap of 0.02\pp{} is consistent with equivalence, but with only 2 seeds the comparison is underpowered (TOST not significant even at $\pm$5\pp{} margin). Per-test classification provides diagnostic information (which specific tests fail) at no cost to variant-level accuracy, a result that parallels process supervision~\cite{lightman2023letsverify}, where step-level verification enables error localization without accuracy cost.

Equivalence is margin-sensitive: at $\pm$1\pp{}, the null hypothesis of difference cannot be rejected ($p = 0.871$); our $\pm$2\pp{} margin reflects the minimal clinically meaningful difference for verification accuracy. Full sensitivity analysis appears in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity}. Oracle analysis on SWE-bench Verified (500 instances) shows that per-test advantage concentrates at $T \geq 2$ fail-to-pass tests (31.3\% of instances, contributing 55.3\% of the total oracle advantage; Appendix~\ref{app:oracle}). \cls{} achieves slightly higher accuracy than \traj{} across both model families (gap 2.1--2.7\pp{}).


\subsection{Repo-Level: Complexity-Dependent Divergence}
\label{sec:repo}

Table~\ref{tab:repo_level} compares \cls{} and \cwm{} on SWE-bench Verified per-test classification at repository-level complexity. Checkpoints are val-selected where validation loss is computable (see \S\ref{sec:setup}); the test set ($n{=}9{,}784$) has zero problem overlap with validation. We report both overall accuracy (where unparseable outputs default to the majority class, pass) and parseable-only accuracy to disentangle generation quality from default assignment effects.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Repo-level cross-formulation comparison on SWE-bench Verified test set ($n{=}9{,}784$). \cwm{} unparseable outputs default to pass (majority class). Seeds: Qwen3 primary seed 42; DeepSeek \traj{} 6 seeds.}
\label{tab:repo_level}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{\cls{} Acc (\%)} & \textbf{\cwm{} Acc (\%)} & \textbf{\cwm{} Unparse (\%)} & \textbf{\traj{} Acc (\%)\textsuperscript{$\ddagger$}} \\
\midrule
Qwen3-4B & \textbf{83.63} & 58.04 & 53.70 & 72.92 \\
Qwen3-8B & 84.48 / 85.60\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & 65.17 & 73.80 & 72.92\textsuperscript{$\diamond$} \\
DeepSeek-6.7B & 82.61 / 74.93\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & 56.70 & 51.00 & 75.35 $\pm$ 5.00 \\
\midrule
0-shot (Qwen3-8B) & 49.90 & --- & --- & --- \\
0-shot (DeepSeek-6.7B) & 48.50 & --- & 8.70 & --- \\
Majority baseline & \multicolumn{4}{c}{72.96} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}

